Lets attempt to think from first principles. 

We have a 16-bit CPU that can sequentially take in 16 bit binary numbers and 
process them one at a time. 

Something like 1110001010101001

Now in this one binary number need to be encoded a bunch of instructions and 
values in agreement with the architecture of the CPU itself. The contract for
how a binary number needs to be loaded with instructions should be something 
that the CPU's API provides.

So based on the hardware implementation of the CPU architecture and the 
decisions taken for the same, we should have a spec.

The hack instruction set has two instructions. A and C. 

The A instruction is specified like so - 

0 v v v v v v v v v v v v v v v 

So bit 15 being 0 specifies to the computer that this is an A instruction. Bits
14 to 0 encode the 15 bit instruction to be loaded into the A register.	

Now keep in mind that calling this combination of bits an A instruction is 
arbitrary. We can just as well call this a D or a J or a DONKEY instruction.

The other instruction is a C instruction specified like so - 

1 1 1 a c1 c2 c3 c4 c5 c6 d1 d2 d3 j1 j2 j3 

The leftmost bit being 1 tells the machine that this is a C instruction. 
The next two bits are ignored. It doesn't matter what they are, they are not
wired to anything. But i think it's just convention to keep them 1.

The next bit indicates A register when 0 and M register when 1. 

c1 - c6 are ALU control bits and d1 - d3 signify the destination for where to
store the bits. j1 - j3 signify whether or not to jump and under what condition
based on the output from the ALU. 

# Two registers and an alias

It is also important at this point to understand how the the registers work
and how to interact with them and to also point out how the design and 
limitations of the CPU dictate this. 

We have two registers - A and D. And we have an alias M which is simply
RAM[A] so M points to the RAM cell whose address is A, meaning held in the 
A register at the point when M is in use.

A natural thing to wonder is why is this needed at all? Why do we have an 
abstraction at this point where we want to keep things readable and simple?
Why not just address RAM[A] as RAM[A]?

This has to do with the limitations of the CPU architecture. We are working 
with a 16-bit CPU and instructions need to be encoded in these 16 bits. 

Now suppose you want to load the value 5 into RAM[100].

Syntactically, RAM[100] = 5

Now look at the contract for the C instruction which allows us to. The first
bit is needed to tell us the type of the instruction. Bits two and three are
ignored, and bit four indicates whether to use the A or M register for the 
ALU operation. The six control bits c1-c6 specify which ALU operation to 
perform, the destination bits d1-d3 specify which destinations to write the
result to and the j bits specify whether or not to jump to a different address
location afterwards. 

So there is no place to smuggle in another constant like 5, that would require
another 15 bits! We'd need atleast a 31 bit architecture.

And so to overcome this limitation we need two steps. To take the same example
of wanting to set RAM[100] = 5

@5 # Load the value 5 into register A
D=5 # Store the value from register A into the D register
@100 # Load the value 100 into register A
M=D # Set the value of M to the value stored in register D 

Now one might think why can't we write an arbitrary constant to the D 
register like we do for the A register. This has to do with the actual 
hardware implementation of the CPU. The A instruction directly stores a 
value in the A register without any questions. It has dedicated circuitry
for this. If you use an A instruction denoted by 0vvvvvvvvvvvvvvv, then the 
15 bits are stored straight into the register. 

We could introduce D instructions as well. But to be able to load the 16-bit
binary with one additinal instruction would require a change in the hardware
implementation, to have circuitry to store arbitrary constants straight into 
register D. Also keep in mind that we only have 16 bits, so using another bit
for register specification (which we will need if moving from two to three 
instructions) means that the arbitrary constants can now only be 14 bits in 
length, so we will be trading off our max num size for this. Is it worth it?
Maybe, maybe not. I don't know. And that is not the question we are concerned
with at the moment. We have a contract given to us by the hardware, and we 
are implementing the assembly. We are not concerned with redesigning the 
hardware.






























